  "answer_core": "Do not confront the partner, investigate on your own, access systems unusually, or choose a reporting channel by yourself. Make a dated, factual note of exactly what you observed and keep it in a safe place. Share only broad context with a trusted personal support (for example, a spouse) without adding detail or speculation. Reach out promptly to a few independent attorneys who handle workplace‑misconduct or whistleblower‑risk matters so you can get confidential, individualized guidance. Let counsel review the situation and help you determine whether any internal or external reporting path is appropriate and when. When you speak with prospective attorneys, ask how they typically decide among internal, audit‑committee, or external routes so you can understand their approach. In the meantime, keep your schedule normal and avoid abrupt behavior changes. If the partner brings up the encounter, keep your response brief and neutral, avoid elaboration, and try not to be alone with them if that is feasible. Move steadily but keep early steps limited and reversible until you have legal guidance.",
